/*
# TextSizeSwitch

The reading-size control: three sizes — smaller, the default, larger — each shown as
the letter A at that size, with the size in use marked. It is a `Segmented`, so it
holds its value the way every segmented control does, Tab reaches it as one stop, and
the arrows move between the sizes.

The value is a scale, one of `sizes`. An app keeps the scale it reads its text at and
binds the switch to it:

```declare-fragment
App [ fontScale: number = 1,
    TextSizeSwitch [ value = { app.fontScale },
        input(v: object) { app.fontScale = v as number } ],
    Text [ fontSize = { 15 * app.fontScale }, text = "Read me" ]
    ]
```

## sizes
The three scales, smallest first, the default in the middle: `[0.9, 1, 1.2]` unless the
app reads at another default.

## choices
One segment per size, each showing its A and named for a screen reader by what it does.

## accessibilityLabel
"Text size": the group's name.

## width
Three segments of 34 each, so the letters keep their room whatever the theme's
segment minimum.
*/

include [ "segmented.declare", "icons/textsize.declare" ]

// the segments name their letters in data, so the build keeps them by name

use [ TextSmallIcon, TextMediumIcon, TextLargeIcon ]

class TextSizeSwitch extends Segmented [
    sizes: number[] = [0.9, 1, 1.2],
    choices = { [ ({ id: classroot.sizes[0], icon: "TextSmallIcon", label: "Smaller text" }),
                  ({ id: classroot.sizes[1], icon: "TextMediumIcon", label: "Default text size" }),
                  ({ id: classroot.sizes[2], icon: "TextLargeIcon", label: "Larger text" }) ] },
    accessibilityLabel = "Text size",
    width = 102
    ]
